.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt ZRAM.CONF 5
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm zram.conf
.Nd configuration for Sowa's compressed swap in RAM
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/sowa/zram.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/zram
sources this file as shell, so each setting is an ordinary assignment and
anything after a
.Sq #
is a comment.
A file that is missing or unreadable is not an error: the service carries the
same defaults the shipped file states.
.Pp
zram is a block device whose contents are kept compressed in memory.
Swapping to one does not move a page out of RAM, it makes the page take about a
third of the room, so a machine with no disk - or none it is allowed to write
to - still has somewhere to put what it is not using.
That is the live medium's case exactly: its root is a tmpfs over the squashfs,
and tmpfs pages are swappable.
.Pp
The service is on in runlevels 2 through 5, first to start and last to stop.
.Xr chkconfig 8
turns it off, and
.Xr service 8
applies a change to this file with
.Cm restart .
.Bl -tag -width ZRAM_SWAPPINESS
.It Ev ZRAM_SIZE
How much uncompressed data the device will accept, written either as a
percentage of this machine's memory or as a size with a suffix
.Pq Dq 2G , Dq 512M , Dq 1GiB .
The default is
.Dq 50% .
.Pp
It is a ceiling and not a reservation.
An empty zram device occupies nothing and a full one occupies whatever its
contents compressed to, so half of memory costs something nearer a sixth of it
once real anonymous pages are in there.
That is also why a figure above 100% is a legitimate thing to ask for, and why
it is the interesting way to run a machine out of memory.
.It Ev ZRAM_ALGORITHM
The compressor, default
.Dq zstd .
This kernel is built with zstd, lzo and lzo-rle;
.Xr zramctl 8
reports what the device ended up with, and
.Pa /sys/block/zram0/comp_algorithm
lists what it could have had.
.It Ev ZRAM_PRIORITY
Swap priority, default 100.
Higher is used first, and 100 is above anything an
.Xr fstab 5
entry would give a disk, so a machine with both fills the compressed swap
before it spills onto the slow one.
.It Ev ZRAM_SWAPPINESS
What
.Pa /proc/sys/vm/swappiness
is set to while the service is up, from 0 to 200; default 100.
The kernel's own default of 60 is written for a device with a seek time, and
swapping here costs a compression instead.
The service records the value it found in
.Pa /run/zram.swappiness
and puts it back when it stops.
Leaving this empty leaves the kernel's setting alone.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /run/zram.swappiness -compact
.It Pa /etc/sowa/zram.conf
this file
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/zram
the service that reads it
.It Pa /var/log/zram.log
what
.Xr zramctl 8 ,
.Xr mkswap 8
and
.Xr swapon 8
had to say
.It Pa /run/zram.swappiness
the swappiness in force before the service started
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Ask for as much compressed swap as the machine has memory, and leave the
kernel's swappiness alone:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ZRAM_SIZE=100%
ZRAM_SWAPPINESS=
.Ed
.Pp
Turn it off at the next boot, and now:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chkconfig zram off
service zram stop
.Ed
.Pp
What it is holding, and what that cost:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
service zram status
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr chkconfig 8 ,
.Xr mkswap 8 ,
.Xr service 8 ,
.Xr swapon 8 ,
.Xr zramctl 8
.Pp
.Pa /root/sowa-howto/zram.txt
